2. Secured and monitored web infrastructure - https://www.foobar.com

Flow: the user connects over HTTPS. Firewall 1 filters traffic before the load
balancer, which holds the SSL certificate, decrypts the request and forwards it to
one of the two servers, each protected by its own firewall. A monitoring client on
each machine sends logs and metrics to an external monitoring service.

Why each additional element:
- 3 firewalls: one in front of the load balancer and one in front of each server,
  so each machine only accepts the traffic it needs.
- 1 SSL certificate: lets www.foobar.com be served over HTTPS.
- 3 monitoring clients: one per machine, collecting data to send to the monitoring
  service (Sumo Logic or similar).

What firewalls are for: they filter network traffic with rules (allowed IPs, ports
and protocols). For example, only ports 80/443 open to the public and 22 only from
the admin's IP. Everything else is blocked.

Why traffic is served over HTTPS: HTTPS encrypts the traffic between the browser and
the site with SSL/TLS, so passwords and personal data cannot be read or altered in
transit, and the certificate proves the site really is www.foobar.com.

What monitoring is used for: to know the health and performance of the
infrastructure (uptime, CPU, memory, disk, response time, errors) and to get an
alert before or when something breaks.

How the monitoring tool collects data: an agent (the monitoring client) installed on
each machine reads logs and system/application metrics and sends them to the
monitoring service, which stores them, builds dashboards and triggers alerts.

How to monitor the web server QPS: have the monitoring client collect Nginx's
request data (the access log, or the stub_status module's request counter), send it
to the monitoring service, and build a dashboard plus an alert on requests per
second.

Issues:
- Terminating SSL at the load balancer: traffic between the load balancer and the
  servers travels unencrypted inside the network, so anyone who gets into that
  network can read it.
- Only one MySQL server accepting writes: it is a SPOF for writes. If the Primary
  fails, the application can no longer save data until someone fixes it or promotes
  the Replica.
- Servers with all the same components: database, web server and application server
  compete for the same CPU and RAM, cannot be scaled separately, and a problem in one
  component (for example the database filling the disk) takes down the others on the
  same machine.
